Additional permission under GNU AGPL version 3 section 7
========================================================

Mirana Technologies Inc. grants an additional permission, under section 7 of
the GNU Affero General Public License version 3, for distribution of this
program in object-code form through Google Play: compliance with the Google
Play terms of service (including the Developer Distribution Agreement and
the Developer Program Policies) is not to be considered a further
restriction under section 7 with respect to (a) material whose copyright is
held by Mirana Technologies Inc., and (b) contributions whose authors
granted Mirana Technologies Inc. the channel-generic store permission with
regrant authority under CONTRIBUTING.md term 2 — this file is that
authority exercised for Google Play, as that term provides. Mirana
Technologies Inc. will not treat Google Play distribution of official
builds as a violation of the license with respect to that material.

A note on why this file exists at all, given that the GPL-family tension
with Play is historically weaker than with the Apple App Store — Signal,
F-Droid projects and many GPL applications distribute on Play. Weaker is not
absent: the Developer Distribution Agreement imposes terms a reader could
treat as "further restrictions", and this file resolves that doubt for
Mirana's material the same way `COPYING.iOS` resolves it for the App Store,
rather than relying on the tension staying weak.


WHAT THIS PERMISSION DOES NOT COVER
-----------------------------------

Read this part. It is the reason this file exists as prose rather than as a
paragraph nobody reads.

Section 7 permits an additional permission only over material for which the
grantor can give copyright permission. **This permission therefore covers
only material for which Mirana Technologies Inc. can give it: material
Mirana holds copyright in, and contributions whose authors granted the
channel-generic store permission with regrant authority under
CONTRIBUTING.md term 2. It does not, and cannot, extend to any other
third-party material.**

The most important of those is **libsignal** (Copyright Signal Messenger,
LLC), which is licensed AGPL-3.0-only and is linked by the iOS application,
the Android application, the command-line client, and the server's
authentication function. Signal granted an App Store additional permission
once, in June 2016, for the now-archived `libsignal-protocol-c` under GPLv3.
That grant was never carried forward to the current libsignal, whose README
states that use outside of Signal is unsupported, and no analogous grant
exists for Google Play or any other channel. Signal distributing its own
Android application — with libsignal inside — on Google Play is its right as
copyright holder and creates no permission for anyone else. **There is no
current permission from Signal to rely on.**

Consequently this file is not a complete answer to the GPL-family/store
question for a binary containing libsignal. It resolves that question only
for the material this permission covers — Mirana's own code, and
contributions regranted under CONTRIBUTING.md term 2. That residual risk
was weighed deliberately for this channel too; the fresh written
acceptance, recorded in the project's internal licensing record by Mirana
Technologies Inc. — the party able to act on it — reads: "I accept the
licensing risk of distributing Tacendum through Google Play — accepted in
writing on 2026-08-20, Natnael Mengesha, Mirana Technologies Inc."

Anyone redistributing a modified version should reach their own conclusion
rather than inheriting ours.


CONTRIBUTIONS
-------------

This permission can only be granted over code the project holds the rights
to. Contributions are accepted on the terms in CONTRIBUTING.md: each
contributor grants Mirana Technologies Inc., over the contribution itself,
a channel-generic store permission — distribution of the combined work
through any app-store distribution channel Mirana designates, today the
Apple App Store and Google Play — **together with the authority for Mirana
to regrant that permission over the contribution as part of the combined
work**, which is what this file does for Google Play. That term was
rewritten on 2026-08-16 from an Apple-only grant incorporated by reference
from `COPYING.iOS`; under its transitional rule, any contribution
submitted before the term's adoption (the 2026-08-16 commit that
introduced it) — merged or not — was assented under the Apple-only term
and requires the submitter's re-assent before it may be merged or
distributed under the widened grant. The stakes are the usual
ones: a single distributed contribution without the grant makes this
permission unretrofittable without tracing every contributor, which is a
multi-year problem and not a paperwork one.
